# =============================================================================
# vor — Caddyfile (example)
# =============================================================================
# THE SECURITY CENTERPIECE OF THE STACK. Read the whole file before editing.
#
# Copy to `Caddyfile`. It defines TWO sites, both proxying the same Plausible app
# (plausible:8000) but exposing very different surfaces:
#
#   1. PUBLIC site   — {$ANALYTICS_DOMAIN}, world-reachable on :80/:443 (bound to
#                      the droplet's PUBLIC IP in docker-compose.yml). Serves ONLY
#                      the tracking script (/js/*) and the event API (/api/event);
#                      everything else returns 404. TLS via Let's Encrypt (ACME).
#
#   2. DASHBOARD site — the admin UI. Caddy serves it as PLAIN HTTP on :8443,
#                      published ONLY on 127.0.0.1 in docker-compose.yml, and
#                      fronted by `tailscale serve` which terminates real HTTPS
#                      (a trusted Tailscale cert for the droplet's MagicDNS name)
#                      and forwards to it over the loopback. So it's reachable
#                      ONLY over the tailnet — never from the public internet.
#
# ── WHY THIS SHAPE (learned the hard way at the live deploy) ────────────────
# Plausible's login enforces three things at once: a CSRF check (request Origin
# must match), a session cookie (Secure cookie needs HTTPS), and a LiveView
# origin check (wants https). Its BASE_URL is the public https domain. Serving
# the dashboard as plain http, or as https on a bare tailnet IP, can't satisfy
# all three — and Caddy's internal CA won't even issue a cert for a bare IP. The
# working answer: let Tailscale Serve provide a REAL https cert on the droplet's
# MagicDNS name, and have Caddy rewrite the upstream headers (below) so Plausible
# sees a request that matches its BASE_URL. Scheme, host, cert, and cookie all
# line up, so login/LiveView work. See docs/DASHBOARD-ACCESS.md.
#
# Validate:
#   docker run --rm -e ANALYTICS_DOMAIN=analytics.example.com \
#     -e ACME_EMAIL=admin@example.com \
#     -v "$PWD/Caddyfile:/etc/caddy/Caddyfile:ro" \
#     caddy:2-alpine caddy validate --config /etc/caddy/Caddyfile
# =============================================================================

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Global options
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
{
	# ACME contact for the public certificate (set via {$ACME_EMAIL} in compose).
	email {$ACME_EMAIL}
}

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 1) PUBLIC ingestion site — only /js/* and /api/event are exposed
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Listens on the public 80/443 (bound to the droplet's public IP in compose).
# Caddy auto-provisions a Let's Encrypt cert for {$ANALYTICS_DOMAIN}.
{$ANALYTICS_DOMAIN} {
	# The only public surface: the tracking script and the event API.
	@public path /js/* /api/event /api/event/

	handle @public {
		reverse_proxy plausible:8000
	}

	# Everything else on the public hostname is refused — the dashboard, login,
	# settings, and stats API live behind Tailscale and must never be reachable
	# from the public internet. 404 (not 403) gives nothing away.
	handle {
		respond "Not found" 404
	}
}

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 2) DASHBOARD site — plain HTTP on :8443, fronted by Tailscale Serve
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# compose publishes :8443 on 127.0.0.1 only, so nothing off-host can reach it
# directly. `tailscale serve --bg --https=443 http://127.0.0.1:8443` provides the
# real HTTPS cert (for the droplet's MagicDNS name) and forwards here.
#
# The header rewrites are the crux: they make Plausible (BASE_URL = the public
# https domain) accept the request even though it arrived over the tailnet, so
# CSRF, the Secure session cookie, and the LiveView origin check all pass.
# X-Forwarded-Proto https is set by `tailscale serve`; we assert it here too.
http://:8443 {
	reverse_proxy plausible:8000 {
		header_up Host {$ANALYTICS_DOMAIN}
		header_up X-Forwarded-Proto https
		header_up Origin https://{$ANALYTICS_DOMAIN}
	}
}
